### Which Maps allow null keys?

| Map Type | Null key | Null value |
|---|---|---|
| HashMap | 1 allowed (bucket 0) | Multiple allowed |
| LinkedHashMap | 1 allowed (bucket 0) | Multiple allowed |
| TreeMap | Not allowed (NPE) | Multiple allowed |
| Hashtable | Not allowed (NPE) | Not allowed (NPE) |
| ConcurrentHashMap | Not allowed (NPE) | Not allowed (NPE) |
| EnumMap | Not allowed (NPE) | Multiple allowed |
| IdentityHashMap | 1 allowed | Multiple allowed |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class NullKeyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // HashMap: null key goes to bucket 0
        Map<String, BigDecimal> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        hashMap.put(null, new BigDecimal("1000000"));
        hashMap.put("SAV2001", null);  // null value OK
        System.out.println("HashMap null key: " + hashMap.get(null));
        System.out.println("HashMap null value: " + hashMap.get("SAV2001"));

        // LinkedHashMap: same as HashMap
        Map<String, BigDecimal> linkedMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        linkedMap.put(null, BigDecimal.ZERO);
        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap null key: " + linkedMap.get(null));

        // TreeMap: null key throws NPE
        Map<String, BigDecimal> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
        treeMap.put("SAV2001", null); // null value OK
        try {
            treeMap.put(null, BigDecimal.ZERO); // NPE!
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("TreeMap null key: NullPointerException");
        }

        // ConcurrentHashMap: no null key or value
        Map<String, BigDecimal> chm = new java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        try {
            chm.put(null, BigDecimal.ZERO);
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("CHM null key: NullPointerException");
        }
        try {
            chm.put("SAV2001", null);
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("CHM null value: NullPointerException");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
HashMap null key: 1000000
HashMap null value: null
LinkedHashMap null key: 0
TreeMap null key: NullPointerException
CHM null key: NullPointerException
CHM null value: NullPointerException
```

### Why does HashMap store null key in bucket 0?

HashMap's internal `hash()` method special-cases null:

```java
static final int hash(Object key) {
    return (key == null) ? 0 : (h = key.hashCode()) ^ (h >>> 16);
}
```

When the key is null, the hash is 0. Bucket index = `0 & (capacity - 1)` = 0. So the null key always goes to bucket 0.

### Why does TreeMap reject null keys?

TreeMap uses `compareTo()` (or a Comparator's `compare()`) to position entries in the Red-Black tree. When you call `null.compareTo(anything)`, Java throws `NullPointerException`. There is no natural ordering position for null.

The exception occurs on the second `put()`, not the first. The first entry has nothing to compare against and is placed at the root. The second entry must be compared with the root key, and if either is null, the comparison fails.

```java
// TreeMap internal put logic (simplified)
public V put(K key, V value) {
    Entry<K,V> t = root;
    if (t == null) {
        // First entry: no comparison needed
        root = new Entry<>(key, value, null);
        return null;
    }
    int cmp;
    Entry<K,V> parent;
    Comparator<? super K> cpr = comparator;
    if (cpr != null) {
        // Comparator: cpr.compare(key, t.key) – NPE if key is null!
    } else {
        // Natural ordering: ((Comparable<K>)key).compareTo(t.key) – NPE if key is null!
    }
}
```

### How do you allow null keys in TreeMap?

Use `Comparator.nullsFirst()` or `Comparator.nullsLast()` to define where null should sort:

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class TreeMapNullWorkaround {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Null sorts before all other keys
        TreeMap<String, String> nullFirst = new TreeMap<>(
            Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder())
        );
        nullFirst.put(null, "Default");
        nullFirst.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
        nullFirst.put("CUR3001", "Priya");
        System.out.println("nullsFirst: " + nullFirst);

        // Null sorts after all other keys
        TreeMap<String, String> nullLast = new TreeMap<>(
            Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder())
        );
        nullLast.put(null, "Default");
        nullLast.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
        System.out.println("nullsLast: " + nullLast);
    }
}
```

```text
nullsFirst: {null=Default, CUR3001=Priya, SAV2001=Rajesh}
nullsLast: {SAV2001=Rajesh, null=Default}
```

### What is the best practice for null handling in maps?

Avoid null keys and null values. Use sentinel objects or `Optional` instead.

```java
// Instead of null key:
private static final String DEFAULT_KEY = "__DEFAULT__";
map.put(DEFAULT_KEY, defaultValue);

// Instead of null value:
map.put("SAV2001", Optional.empty()); // explicit "no value"
// Or use getOrDefault:
BigDecimal balance = map.getOrDefault("UNKNOWN", BigDecimal.ZERO);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Migration risk.** Code that uses HashMap with null keys will break when migrated to ConcurrentHashMap or TreeMap. Always search for null key usage before switching map implementations.

- **containsKey vs get for null.** `get(key)` returning null is ambiguous: key absent or value is null. Use `containsKey(key)` to distinguish. But in concurrent maps, this check-then-act is racy, which is why ConcurrentHashMap rejects null entirely.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How does HashMap store the null key?" – Hash 0, bucket 0.
- "Why does TreeMap reject null keys?" – compareTo throws NPE on null.
- "Why does ConcurrentHashMap reject null values?" – get() returning null would be ambiguous.
- "How do you allow null keys in TreeMap?" – Comparator.nullsFirst() or nullsLast().
